---
title: Managed MCP with chat()
id: mcp-managed
order: 9
description: "Hand live MCP clients and pools to chat() via the mcp option and let it own tool discovery and connection lifecycle for you."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - mcp
  - model context protocol
  - chat mcp
  - mcp clients
  - keep-alive
  - lazyTools
  - onDiscoveryError
  - toolFilter
  - needsApproval
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have one or more live "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP clients"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (or pools) and you want the model to use their tools — without writing boilerplate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await client.tools()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"try/finally close()"},{"type":"text","value":" blocks for every route. By the end of this guide you'll hand those clients to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" option and let it handle both discovery and lifecycle for you."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" tries protocol "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2026-07-28"},{"type":"text","value":" first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package names for an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk"},{"type":"text","value":" import are in "},{"type":"link","href":"../migration/mcp-sdk","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP SDK packages"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" prop) vs manual ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":" spread)"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp: { clients: [...] }"},{"type":"text","value":" when you want "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"discovery + lifecycle"}]},{"type":"text","value":" managed for you and you are happy with runtime-typed ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":"-argument) tools."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools: [...await client.tools([toolDefinition(...)])]"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fully-typed MCP tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — the defs overload gives you Zod-validated, TypeScript-typed arguments. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-manual","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp#three-modes-of-type-safety","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three Modes of Type Safety"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both coexist in the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call. Tools from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp.clients"},{"type":"text","value":" are merged with any tools you pass explicitly via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hand-a-client-to-chat","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hand a client to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest path: create a client, hand it to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":", and let the run clean it up. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'close'"},{"type":"text","value":", so the client is closed automatically once the run ends — on success, error, or abort."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/routes/api.chat.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        const mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: {\n            type: 'http',\n            url: process.env.MCP_URL!,\n            headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n          },\n        })\n\n        // chat() discovers mcpClient's tools and closes the connection when done.\n        // No try/finally needed.\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: {\n            clients: [mcpClient],\n            // connection: 'close' is the default — shown here for clarity\n            connection: 'close',\n          },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The examples below show only the part that changes — the client setup and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call. They all drop into the same route handler shape as above."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"multiple-servers-and-pools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple servers and pools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass any mix of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" instances and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":" pools. Their tools are discovered in parallel and merged into one flat tool set. Pools auto-prefix each server's tools with the config key to prevent name collisions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient, createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// A pool of two servers — their tools are prefixed \"github_\" and \"linear_\"\nconst githubLinearPool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.GITHUB_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n  linear: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.LINEAR_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// A standalone client for an internal server\nconst internalClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.INTERNAL_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\n// All three servers' tools are merged: github_*, linear_*, plus internal tools\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [githubLinearPool, internalClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limit-which-tools-the-model-gets","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limit which tools the model gets"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ mcp })"},{"type":"text","value":" sends every tool that each client discovers. To hide tools, or to require approval, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" on the client. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the options of each client:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient, createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// One client: send only read-only tools\nconst github = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n})\n\n// A pool: each server gets its own options\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  linear: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! },\n    needsApproval: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint !== true,\n  },\n  docs: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.DOCS_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: { clients: [github, pool] },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hints come from the server, and a compromised server can send false hints. For the trust rules and a name-based filter, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp#limit-and-gate-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limit and Gate Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"keep-connections-warm","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep connections warm"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating a new MCP connection on every request adds latency. For production routes with high request rates, create your pool once at module level and pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection: 'keep-alive'"},{"type":"text","value":" so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" never closes it. The pool stays ready for the next request. (Shown as a full route because the placement — module scope vs. handler scope — is the point.)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server route ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/routes/api.chat.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"):"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\n// Created once when the module loads. Shared across all requests.\nconst sharedPool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.GITHUB_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n  linear: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.LINEAR_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        // keep-alive: sharedPool is never closed by chat(); stays warm for next call\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: {\n            clients: [sharedPool],\n            connection: 'keep-alive',\n          },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client component ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/components/Chat.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":"):"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n}\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, status } = useChat(chatOptions)\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <ul>\n        {messages.map((m) => (\n          <li key={m.id}>\n            <strong>{m.role}:</strong>{' '}\n            {m.parts.find((p) => p.type === 'text')?.content}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n      <button\n        onClick={() => sendMessage({ content: 'List my open GitHub issues' })}\n        disabled={status === 'streaming'}\n      >\n        Ask\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"lazy-tool-discovery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy tool discovery"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When your MCP server exposes dozens of tools, sending every schema to the model inflates prompt size and cost. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazyTools: true"},{"type":"text","value":" to defer sending tool schemas until the model explicitly requests them."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LARGE_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [mcpClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n    // Tools are registered but schemas are withheld until the model asks\n    lazyTools: true,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazyTools: true"},{"type":"text","value":" is forwarded to each source's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools({ lazy: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" call. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./lazy-tool-discovery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy Tool Discovery"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for how the model discovers and loads lazy tools at runtime, and "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp#lazy-tool-discovery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the standalone lazy discovery section"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ lazy: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" directly with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client.tools()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"handling-discovery-failures","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling discovery failures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, if any source fails during discovery, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" throws immediately (fail-fast). When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection: 'close'"},{"type":"text","value":", any sources that did connect are cleaned up before the error propagates — no leaked connections."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fail-fast (default):"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.MCP_URL! },\n})\n\n// If discovery fails, chat() throws before the first model call.\n// mcpClient is closed automatically (connection: 'close' default).\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [mcpClient],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skip a flaky server and proceed:"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDiscoveryError"},{"type":"text","value":" to log the problem and return normally — the failing source is skipped and the run continues with the remaining clients' tools."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst primaryClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.PRIMARY_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst optionalClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.OPTIONAL_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [primaryClient, optionalClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n    onDiscoveryError(error, source) {\n      // Log the failure but let the run proceed without this source's tools.\n      // Throw here (or re-throw `error`) to fail the whole run instead.\n      console.warn('MCP discovery failed for a source, skipping.', error)\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sources passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDiscoveryError"},{"type":"text","value":" may have already connected before discovery failed. When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection: 'close'"},{"type":"text","value":", they are still closed at the end of the run — even if their tools were skipped."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-name-collisions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool name collisions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If two sources in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp.clients"},{"type":"text","value":" expose a tool with the same name, the run fails with an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPDuplicateToolNameError"},{"type":"text","value":" (exported from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai"},{"type":"text","value":") after merging the discovered tools. Note that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs lazily — discovery happens when the stream is first consumed, so the error surfaces "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"through the stream"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (the SSE response errors), not as a synchronous throw you can "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"try/catch"},{"type":"text","value":" at the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call site. The fix is to prevent the collision up front: assign a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prefix"},{"type":"text","value":" to one of the clients, or use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":" (which auto-prefixes using the config key)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// Both servers expose a tool called \"search\". Without prefixes the run\n// would fail with MCPDuplicateToolNameError. The prefix option resolves\n// the clash.\nconst serverA = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.SERVER_A_URL! },\n  prefix: 'alpha', // tools become \"alpha_search\", etc.\n})\n\nconst serverB = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.SERVER_B_URL! },\n  prefix: 'beta', // tools become \"beta_search\", etc.\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [serverA, serverB],\n    connection: 'close',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the standalone "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pool.tools()"},{"type":"text","value":" collision behavior and the general "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prefix"},{"type":"text","value":" strategy, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp#tool-name-collisions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Name Collisions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp#disable-or-override-the-prefix","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable or override the prefix"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"going-further","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Going further"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Need fully-typed tools, resources, or prompts in the run?"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" prop gives you runtime-typed tools and discovery. To spread "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":"-typed MCP tools, inject MCP resources and prompts, or cancel in-flight MCP calls, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-manual","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"hand-a-client-to-chat","text":"Hand a client to chat()","level":2},{"id":"multiple-servers-and-pools","text":"Multiple servers and pools","level":2},{"id":"limit-which-tools-the-model-gets","text":"Limit which tools the model gets","level":2},{"id":"keep-connections-warm","text":"Keep connections warm","level":2},{"id":"lazy-tool-discovery","text":"Lazy tool discovery","level":2},{"id":"handling-discovery-failures","text":"Handling discovery failures","level":2},{"id":"tool-name-collisions","text":"Tool name collisions","level":2},{"id":"going-further","text":"Going further","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou have one or more live [MCP clients](./mcp) (or pools) and you want the model to use their tools — without writing boilerplate `await client.tools()` calls and `try/finally close()` blocks for every route. By the end of this guide you'll hand those clients to `chat()` via the `mcp` option and let it handle both discovery and lifecycle for you.\n\n> `createMCPClient` tries protocol `2026-07-28` first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake.\n>\n> Package names for an `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` import are in [MCP SDK packages](../migration/mcp-sdk).\n\n> **Managed (`mcp` prop) vs manual (`tools` spread)**\n>\n> - Use `mcp: { clients: [...] }` when you want **discovery + lifecycle** managed for you and you are happy with runtime-typed (`unknown`-argument) tools.\n> - Use `tools: [...await client.tools([toolDefinition(...)])]` when you need **fully-typed MCP tools** — the defs overload gives you Zod-validated, TypeScript-typed arguments. See [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual) and [Three Modes of Type Safety](./mcp#three-modes-of-type-safety).\n>\n> Both coexist in the same `chat()` call. Tools from `mcp.clients` are merged with any tools you pass explicitly via `tools`.\n\n## Hand a client to `chat()`\n\nThe simplest path: create a client, hand it to `chat()`, and let the run clean it up. `connection` defaults to `'close'`, so the client is closed automatically once the run ends — on success, error, or abort.\n\n```ts ignore\n// src/routes/api.chat.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        const mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: {\n            type: 'http',\n            url: process.env.MCP_URL!,\n            headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n          },\n        })\n\n        // chat() discovers mcpClient's tools and closes the connection when done.\n        // No try/finally needed.\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: {\n            clients: [mcpClient],\n            // connection: 'close' is the default — shown here for clarity\n            connection: 'close',\n          },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe examples below show only the part that changes — the client setup and the `chat()` call. They all drop into the same route handler shape as above.\n\n## Multiple servers and pools\n\nPass any mix of `MCPClient` instances and `MCPClients` pools. Their tools are discovered in parallel and merged into one flat tool set. Pools auto-prefix each server's tools with the config key to prevent name collisions.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient, createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// A pool of two servers — their tools are prefixed \"github_\" and \"linear_\"\nconst githubLinearPool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.GITHUB_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n  linear: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.LINEAR_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// A standalone client for an internal server\nconst internalClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.INTERNAL_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\n// All three servers' tools are merged: github_*, linear_*, plus internal tools\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [githubLinearPool, internalClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Limit which tools the model gets\n\n`chat({ mcp })` sends every tool that each client discovers. To hide tools, or to require approval, set `toolFilter` or `needsApproval` on the client. `chat()` uses the options of each client:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient, createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// One client: send only read-only tools\nconst github = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n})\n\n// A pool: each server gets its own options\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  linear: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! },\n    needsApproval: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint !== true,\n  },\n  docs: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.DOCS_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: { clients: [github, pool] },\n})\n```\n\nThe hints come from the server, and a compromised server can send false hints. For the trust rules and a name-based filter, see [Limit and Gate Tools](./mcp#limit-and-gate-tools).\n\n## Keep connections warm\n\nCreating a new MCP connection on every request adds latency. For production routes with high request rates, create your pool once at module level and pass `connection: 'keep-alive'` so `chat()` never closes it. The pool stays ready for the next request. (Shown as a full route because the placement — module scope vs. handler scope — is the point.)\n\n**Server route (`src/routes/api.chat.ts`):**\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\n// Created once when the module loads. Shared across all requests.\nconst sharedPool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.GITHUB_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n  linear: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.LINEAR_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        // keep-alive: sharedPool is never closed by chat(); stays warm for next call\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: {\n            clients: [sharedPool],\n            connection: 'keep-alive',\n          },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**Client component (`src/components/Chat.tsx`):**\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n}\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, status } = useChat(chatOptions)\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <ul>\n        {messages.map((m) => (\n          <li key={m.id}>\n            <strong>{m.role}:</strong>{' '}\n            {m.parts.find((p) => p.type === 'text')?.content}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n      <button\n        onClick={() => sendMessage({ content: 'List my open GitHub issues' })}\n        disabled={status === 'streaming'}\n      >\n        Ask\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Lazy tool discovery\n\nWhen your MCP server exposes dozens of tools, sending every schema to the model inflates prompt size and cost. Set `lazyTools: true` to defer sending tool schemas until the model explicitly requests them.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LARGE_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [mcpClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n    // Tools are registered but schemas are withheld until the model asks\n    lazyTools: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`lazyTools: true` is forwarded to each source's `tools({ lazy: true })` call. See [Lazy Tool Discovery](./lazy-tool-discovery) for how the model discovers and loads lazy tools at runtime, and [the standalone lazy discovery section](./mcp#lazy-tool-discovery) for using `{ lazy: true }` directly with `client.tools()`.\n\n## Handling discovery failures\n\nBy default, if any source fails during discovery, `chat()` throws immediately (fail-fast). When `connection: 'close'`, any sources that did connect are cleaned up before the error propagates — no leaked connections.\n\n**Fail-fast (default):**\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.MCP_URL! },\n})\n\n// If discovery fails, chat() throws before the first model call.\n// mcpClient is closed automatically (connection: 'close' default).\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [mcpClient],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**Skip a flaky server and proceed:**\n\nUse `onDiscoveryError` to log the problem and return normally — the failing source is skipped and the run continues with the remaining clients' tools.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst primaryClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.PRIMARY_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst optionalClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.OPTIONAL_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [primaryClient, optionalClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n    onDiscoveryError(error, source) {\n      // Log the failure but let the run proceed without this source's tools.\n      // Throw here (or re-throw `error`) to fail the whole run instead.\n      console.warn('MCP discovery failed for a source, skipping.', error)\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> Sources passed to `onDiscoveryError` may have already connected before discovery failed. When `connection: 'close'`, they are still closed at the end of the run — even if their tools were skipped.\n\n## Tool name collisions\n\nIf two sources in `mcp.clients` expose a tool with the same name, the run fails with an `MCPDuplicateToolNameError` (exported from `@tanstack/ai`) after merging the discovered tools. Note that `chat()` runs lazily — discovery happens when the stream is first consumed, so the error surfaces **through the stream** (the SSE response errors), not as a synchronous throw you can `try/catch` at the `chat()` call site. The fix is to prevent the collision up front: assign a `prefix` to one of the clients, or use `createMCPClients` (which auto-prefixes using the config key).\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// Both servers expose a tool called \"search\". Without prefixes the run\n// would fail with MCPDuplicateToolNameError. The prefix option resolves\n// the clash.\nconst serverA = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.SERVER_A_URL! },\n  prefix: 'alpha', // tools become \"alpha_search\", etc.\n})\n\nconst serverB = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.SERVER_B_URL! },\n  prefix: 'beta', // tools become \"beta_search\", etc.\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [serverA, serverB],\n    connection: 'close',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nFor the standalone `pool.tools()` collision behavior and the general `prefix` strategy, see [Tool Name Collisions](./mcp#tool-name-collisions) and [Disable or override the prefix](./mcp#disable-or-override-the-prefix).\n\n## Going further\n\n> **Need fully-typed tools, resources, or prompts in the run?** The `mcp` prop gives you runtime-typed tools and discovery. To spread `toolDefinition`-typed MCP tools, inject MCP resources and prompts, or cancel in-flight MCP calls, see [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Managed MCP with chat()\nid: mcp-managed\norder: 9\ndescription: \"Hand live MCP clients and pools to chat() via the mcp option and let it own tool discovery and connection lifecycle for you.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - mcp\n  - model context protocol\n  - chat mcp\n  - mcp clients\n  - keep-alive\n  - lazyTools\n  - onDiscoveryError\n  - toolFilter\n  - needsApproval\n---\n\nYou have one or more live [MCP clients](./mcp) (or pools) and you want the model to use their tools — without writing boilerplate `await client.tools()` calls and `try/finally close()` blocks for every route. By the end of this guide you'll hand those clients to `chat()` via the `mcp` option and let it handle both discovery and lifecycle for you.\n\n> `createMCPClient` tries protocol `2026-07-28` first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake.\n>\n> Package names for an `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` import are in [MCP SDK packages](../migration/mcp-sdk).\n\n> **Managed (`mcp` prop) vs manual (`tools` spread)**\n>\n> - Use `mcp: { clients: [...] }` when you want **discovery + lifecycle** managed for you and you are happy with runtime-typed (`unknown`-argument) tools.\n> - Use `tools: [...await client.tools([toolDefinition(...)])]` when you need **fully-typed MCP tools** — the defs overload gives you Zod-validated, TypeScript-typed arguments. See [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual) and [Three Modes of Type Safety](./mcp#three-modes-of-type-safety).\n>\n> Both coexist in the same `chat()` call. Tools from `mcp.clients` are merged with any tools you pass explicitly via `tools`.\n\n## Hand a client to `chat()`\n\nThe simplest path: create a client, hand it to `chat()`, and let the run clean it up. `connection` defaults to `'close'`, so the client is closed automatically once the run ends — on success, error, or abort.\n\n```ts ignore\n// src/routes/api.chat.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        const mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: {\n            type: 'http',\n            url: process.env.MCP_URL!,\n            headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n          },\n        })\n\n        // chat() discovers mcpClient's tools and closes the connection when done.\n        // No try/finally needed.\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: {\n            clients: [mcpClient],\n            // connection: 'close' is the default — shown here for clarity\n            connection: 'close',\n          },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe examples below show only the part that changes — the client setup and the `chat()` call. They all drop into the same route handler shape as above.\n\n## Multiple servers and pools\n\nPass any mix of `MCPClient` instances and `MCPClients` pools. Their tools are discovered in parallel and merged into one flat tool set. Pools auto-prefix each server's tools with the config key to prevent name collisions.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient, createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// A pool of two servers — their tools are prefixed \"github_\" and \"linear_\"\nconst githubLinearPool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.GITHUB_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n  linear: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.LINEAR_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// A standalone client for an internal server\nconst internalClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.INTERNAL_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\n// All three servers' tools are merged: github_*, linear_*, plus internal tools\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [githubLinearPool, internalClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Limit which tools the model gets\n\n`chat({ mcp })` sends every tool that each client discovers. To hide tools, or to require approval, set `toolFilter` or `needsApproval` on the client. `chat()` uses the options of each client:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient, createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// One client: send only read-only tools\nconst github = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n})\n\n// A pool: each server gets its own options\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  linear: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! },\n    needsApproval: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint !== true,\n  },\n  docs: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.DOCS_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: { clients: [github, pool] },\n})\n```\n\nThe hints come from the server, and a compromised server can send false hints. For the trust rules and a name-based filter, see [Limit and Gate Tools](./mcp#limit-and-gate-tools).\n\n## Keep connections warm\n\nCreating a new MCP connection on every request adds latency. For production routes with high request rates, create your pool once at module level and pass `connection: 'keep-alive'` so `chat()` never closes it. The pool stays ready for the next request. (Shown as a full route because the placement — module scope vs. handler scope — is the point.)\n\n**Server route (`src/routes/api.chat.ts`):**\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\n// Created once when the module loads. Shared across all requests.\nconst sharedPool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.GITHUB_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n  linear: {\n    transport: {\n      type: 'http',\n      url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL!,\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.LINEAR_MCP_TOKEN}` },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        // keep-alive: sharedPool is never closed by chat(); stays warm for next call\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: {\n            clients: [sharedPool],\n            connection: 'keep-alive',\n          },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**Client component (`src/components/Chat.tsx`):**\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n}\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, status } = useChat(chatOptions)\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <ul>\n        {messages.map((m) => (\n          <li key={m.id}>\n            <strong>{m.role}:</strong>{' '}\n            {m.parts.find((p) => p.type === 'text')?.content}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n      <button\n        onClick={() => sendMessage({ content: 'List my open GitHub issues' })}\n        disabled={status === 'streaming'}\n      >\n        Ask\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Lazy tool discovery\n\nWhen your MCP server exposes dozens of tools, sending every schema to the model inflates prompt size and cost. Set `lazyTools: true` to defer sending tool schemas until the model explicitly requests them.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LARGE_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [mcpClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n    // Tools are registered but schemas are withheld until the model asks\n    lazyTools: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`lazyTools: true` is forwarded to each source's `tools({ lazy: true })` call. See [Lazy Tool Discovery](./lazy-tool-discovery) for how the model discovers and loads lazy tools at runtime, and [the standalone lazy discovery section](./mcp#lazy-tool-discovery) for using `{ lazy: true }` directly with `client.tools()`.\n\n## Handling discovery failures\n\nBy default, if any source fails during discovery, `chat()` throws immediately (fail-fast). When `connection: 'close'`, any sources that did connect are cleaned up before the error propagates — no leaked connections.\n\n**Fail-fast (default):**\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst mcpClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.MCP_URL! },\n})\n\n// If discovery fails, chat() throws before the first model call.\n// mcpClient is closed automatically (connection: 'close' default).\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [mcpClient],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**Skip a flaky server and proceed:**\n\nUse `onDiscoveryError` to log the problem and return normally — the failing source is skipped and the run continues with the remaining clients' tools.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst primaryClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.PRIMARY_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst optionalClient = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.OPTIONAL_MCP_URL! },\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [primaryClient, optionalClient],\n    connection: 'close',\n    onDiscoveryError(error, source) {\n      // Log the failure but let the run proceed without this source's tools.\n      // Throw here (or re-throw `error`) to fail the whole run instead.\n      console.warn('MCP discovery failed for a source, skipping.', error)\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> Sources passed to `onDiscoveryError` may have already connected before discovery failed. When `connection: 'close'`, they are still closed at the end of the run — even if their tools were skipped.\n\n## Tool name collisions\n\nIf two sources in `mcp.clients` expose a tool with the same name, the run fails with an `MCPDuplicateToolNameError` (exported from `@tanstack/ai`) after merging the discovered tools. Note that `chat()` runs lazily — discovery happens when the stream is first consumed, so the error surfaces **through the stream** (the SSE response errors), not as a synchronous throw you can `try/catch` at the `chat()` call site. The fix is to prevent the collision up front: assign a `prefix` to one of the clients, or use `createMCPClients` (which auto-prefixes using the config key).\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\n// Both servers expose a tool called \"search\". Without prefixes the run\n// would fail with MCPDuplicateToolNameError. The prefix option resolves\n// the clash.\nconst serverA = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.SERVER_A_URL! },\n  prefix: 'alpha', // tools become \"alpha_search\", etc.\n})\n\nconst serverB = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.SERVER_B_URL! },\n  prefix: 'beta', // tools become \"beta_search\", etc.\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: {\n    clients: [serverA, serverB],\n    connection: 'close',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nFor the standalone `pool.tools()` collision behavior and the general `prefix` strategy, see [Tool Name Collisions](./mcp#tool-name-collisions) and [Disable or override the prefix](./mcp#disable-or-override-the-prefix).\n\n## Going further\n\n> **Need fully-typed tools, resources, or prompts in the run?** The `mcp` prop gives you runtime-typed tools and discovery. To spread `toolDefinition`-typed MCP tools, inject MCP resources and prompts, or cancel in-flight MCP calls, see [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual).\n"}
